WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:07.430
Neutron stars.

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:12.170
Manhattan sized, but with a mass twice that
of our sun.

00:00:14.650 --> 00:00:15.820
So dense.

00:00:15.850 --> 00:00:18.280
A teaspoon of their matter weighs a billion
tons.

00:00:20.370 --> 00:00:23.310
Mind blowing objects that arrive with a
bang.

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:28.590
Neutron stars spark into life amid the death
of their parent star.

00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:33.030
They are the ultimate story of resurrection
or of life from death.

00:00:34.130 --> 00:00:36.830
It's all part of a cosmic cycle.

00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:41.750
Stars are born from giant clouds of very cold
gas.

00:00:42.410 --> 00:00:47.600
Those clouds collapse under their own
gravity, and the density of the core at the

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:49.970
center of that collapse starts to increase.

00:00:54.860 --> 00:00:58.610
A star is a huge nuclear fusion reactor.

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:04.490
The force of its gravity is so powerful that
it fuses atoms together to make progressively

00:01:04.490 --> 00:01:06.620
heavier and heavier elements.

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:13.110
The star fuses hydrogen into helium once it
exhausts its hydrogen.

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:16.830
Then, if it's massive enough, it can start
fusing helium at its core.

00:01:19.090 --> 00:01:26.770
Fusion continues forming carbon, oxygen,
nitrogen all the way up to iron.

00:01:28.010 --> 00:01:33.800
Once the star has iron in the core, it's
almost like you've poisoned it, because this

00:01:33.830 --> 00:01:37.080
extinguishes the nuclear reactions in the
core of the star.

00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:40.430
You fuse something into iron and you get no
energy.

00:01:41.270 --> 00:01:43.340
All of a sudden, there's nothing to support.

00:01:43.340 --> 00:01:44.510
The crush of gravity.

00:01:44.540 --> 00:01:45.740
No radiation pressure.

00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:50.240
Pushing out means no pressure keeping the
outer regions from falling in.

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:51.500
And that's what they do.

00:01:52.340 --> 00:01:59.060
As the star collapses in its death throes,
its core becomes the wildest, craziest, and

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:01.040
freakiest pressure cooker in the whole
universe.

00:02:05.330 --> 00:02:07.490
The ingredients are all in place.

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:10.790
Time to start cooking up a neutron star.

00:02:12.290 --> 00:02:18.590
If we were to scale up an atomic nucleus to
be the size of a baseball in a normal atom,

00:02:18.590 --> 00:02:23.030
the nearest electron would be way over in
those trees.

00:02:23.030 --> 00:02:28.070
But in the extreme conditions that lead to
the formation of a neutron star, those

00:02:28.070 --> 00:02:30.980
electrons can be pushed closer to the
nucleus.

00:02:31.490 --> 00:02:36.830
They can come zipping in from any direction,
and if the temperatures and pressures are

00:02:36.830 --> 00:02:41.600
high enough, they can even strike the
nucleus and enter it and they can hit a

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:46.880
proton. And when they do, they become
converted into more neutrons.

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:52.130
So in the formation of one of these objects,
the protons and electrons disappear and

00:02:52.130 --> 00:02:56.660
you're left with almost entirely pure
neutrons, with nothing to stop them from

00:02:56.660 --> 00:03:03.560
cramming together and filling up this entire
baseball with neutrons, leading to incredibly

00:03:03.560 --> 00:03:04.820
high densities.

00:03:05.940 --> 00:03:11.450
With the sea of electrons now absorbed into
the atomic nuclei, the matter in the stars

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:14.010
can now press together a lot tighter.

00:03:15.270 --> 00:03:20.850
It's like squeezing 300 million tons of mass
into a single sugar cube.

00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:26.470
As the star collapses, enormous amounts of
gas fall towards the core.

00:03:29.270 --> 00:03:32.450
The core is small in size, but huge in mass.

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:39.870
Billions of tons of gas bounce off of it,
then erupt into the biggest fireworks display

00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:44.540
in the cosmos. A supernova.

00:03:45.790 --> 00:03:49.330
It's massive, it's bright, it's imposing.

00:03:49.330 --> 00:03:54.070
Supernova are among the most dramatic events
to happen in the universe.

00:03:54.070 --> 00:04:00.670
The single star dying, one star dying can
outshine an entire galaxy.

00:04:04.360 --> 00:04:11.050
And arising out of this cataclysm, a new and
very strange cosmic entity.

00:04:13.300 --> 00:04:17.050
When the smoke finally clears from the
supernova explosion, you're left with one of

00:04:17.050 --> 00:04:21.940
the most real, fascinating, unbelievable
monsters in the entire universe.

00:04:21.970 --> 00:04:26.980
Humans have been witnessing supernovas for
thousands of years, but we're only now just

00:04:26.980 --> 00:04:29.500
starting to understand what we've truly been
witnessing.

00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:32.860
The births of neutron stars.

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:41.610
But while supernovas are big and bright,
neutron stars are small, and many don't even

00:04:41.610 --> 00:04:42.690
give off light.

00:04:43.590 --> 00:04:46.680
So how many neutron stars are out there?

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:52.600
We know of about 2000 neutron stars in our
galaxy, but there probably are many, many

00:04:52.600 --> 00:04:57.130
more. I'm talking about tens of millions in
the Milky Way alone, and certainly billions

00:04:57.130 --> 00:04:58.210
throughout the universe.

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:08.370
Neutron stars may be small, but some give
themselves away shooting beams across the

00:05:08.370 --> 00:05:14.220
universe. Unmistakable pulsing strobes of a
cosmic lighthouse.

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:24.390
Our knowledge of neutron stars is expanding
fast.

00:05:28.230 --> 00:05:34.020
But we didn't even know they existed until a
lucky discovery just over 50 years ago.

00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:38.890
Cambridge, the Mullard Radio Observatory.

00:05:38.920 --> 00:05:39.950
Jocelyn Bell.

00:05:39.970 --> 00:05:46.240
Grad student operating the new radio
telescope, scanning the sky, doing all sorts

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:51.250
of cool astronomy stuff and sees what she
calls a bit of scruff in the data.

00:05:52.810 --> 00:05:59.120
This scruff is a short but constantly
repeating burst of radiation originating a

00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:00.850
thousand light years from Earth.

00:06:01.810 --> 00:06:06.700
It's so stable and regular that Bell is
convinced there's something wrong with her

00:06:06.700 --> 00:06:07.510
telescope.

00:06:07.750 --> 00:06:15.880
She returns to that spot and finds a
repeating regular signal, a single point in

00:06:15.880 --> 00:06:22.930
the sky that is flashing at us, continually
saying, hai, hai hai, blip.

00:06:22.930 --> 00:06:24.160
Blip, blip.

00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:26.590
Boom boom boom.

00:06:26.590 --> 00:06:28.330
Pulse pulse pulse.

00:06:28.360 --> 00:06:33.970
Nothing that we know of in the universe has
such a steady, perfectly spaced in time

00:06:33.970 --> 00:06:39.160
pulse. It seems so perfect that it must have
been artificial.

00:06:39.190 --> 00:06:42.130
It looks like someone is making that.

00:06:42.430 --> 00:06:45.910
But it turns out it's not a person, but a
thing.

00:06:46.510 --> 00:06:49.260
What she discovered is called a pulsar.

00:06:52.230 --> 00:06:56.550
A pulsar is a type of rapidly spinning
neutron star.

00:06:59.040 --> 00:07:05.250
Neutron stars had been theorized in the
1930s, but were thought to be too faint to be

00:07:05.250 --> 00:07:06.060
detected.

00:07:06.870 --> 00:07:14.340
Neutron stars were hypothesized to exist, but
not really taken seriously.

00:07:14.340 --> 00:07:16.790
It was just a oh, that's cute.

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:19.440
Maybe they're out there, but probably not.

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:25.050
The signal Bell detected seemed like
something from science fiction.

00:07:26.490 --> 00:07:31.200
No one had ever seen this in astronomy
before, and some people even speculated that

00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:33.180
it was an alien signal.

00:07:33.270 --> 00:07:36.360
She even called them LGM objects little green
men.

00:07:38.310 --> 00:07:41.430
But then Bell found a second signal.

00:07:43.830 --> 00:07:50.070
Little green men went back to being fiction
and pulsars became science fact.

00:07:50.690 --> 00:07:52.790
The discovery of pulsars came out of the
blue.

00:07:53.150 --> 00:07:54.650
Nobody was expecting this.

00:07:54.650 --> 00:07:57.320
So it was an amazing breakthrough, really
important.

00:08:00.950 --> 00:08:04.580
Pulsars pulse because they're born to spin.

00:08:06.510 --> 00:08:10.890
They burst into life as their parents star
collapses during a supernova.

00:08:13.540 --> 00:08:18.040
Any object at all that is undergoing any sort
of compression event.

00:08:18.040 --> 00:08:23.860
If it has any initial angular momentum at
all, it will eventually end up spinning.

00:08:25.600 --> 00:08:28.930
As the star shrinks, it spins faster and
faster.

00:08:31.570 --> 00:08:38.440
They spin so quickly because the earth sized
core of a massive star collapsed to something

00:08:38.440 --> 00:08:39.970
as small as a city.

00:08:40.120 --> 00:08:46.240
So because the size of the object became so
much smaller, the rate of spin had to

00:08:46.240 --> 00:08:47.950
increase by a tremendous amount.

